window.QUIZ_BANK['api-postman'] = (window.QUIZ_BANK['api-postman']||[]).concat([
{"id":2921,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is a \"Filter\" in REST Assured, and what interface must custom filters implement?","options":["An interceptor implementing `io.restassured.filter.Filter` that inspects, transforms, or logs requests and responses before and after execution","A database query parser for filtering SQL rows","A JUnit test runner annotation","A Maven plugin for filtering resources"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A filter in REST Assured implements the `io.restassured.filter.Filter` interface. It provides a powerful interception mechanism allowing developers to inspect, modify, or log requests and responses dynamically across the entire HTTP execution lifecycle."},
{"id":2922,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you attach custom filters to a REST Assured request specification?","options":["given().filter(new CustomAuthFilter()).when().get(\"/api\")","given().withInterceptor(new CustomAuthFilter()).get(\"/api\")","RestAssured.addPlugin(new CustomAuthFilter());","given().interceptor(new CustomAuthFilter()).get(\"/api\")"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Custom filters are attached using `given().filter(filterInstance)` or globally via `RestAssured.filters(filter1, filter2)`. The filter's `filter(requestSpec, responseSpec, ctx)` method is invoked for every request/response cycle. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2923,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which built-in REST Assured filter logs requests and responses directly into an Allure Report framework attachment?","options":["AllureRestAssured filter from `io.qameta.allure:allure-rest-assured`","ExtentRestAssured filter","AllureReportLogger","Log4jRestAssuredFilter"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The `AllureRestAssured` filter (provided by the `allure-rest-assured` artifact) intercepts HTTP transactions and automatically generates formatted, syntax-highlighted request and response attachments within Allure test reports. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2924,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you structure a REST Assured test suite using TestNG annotations for lifecycle setup and execution?","options":["Use `@BeforeClass` or `@BeforeMethod` to set `RestAssured.baseURI` and common specs, and `@Test` for individual test cases","Use `@RunWith(Suite.class)` only","Use `@Step` on static main methods","Use `@TestNGSuite` inside XML config only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a standard TestNG setup, `@BeforeClass` or `@BeforeMethod` configures `RestAssured.baseURI`, `basePath`, and common `RequestSpecification` instances, while `@Test` methods execute and assert individual endpoint behaviors independently. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2925,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How can you run data-driven REST Assured tests in parallel using TestNG?","options":["Use `@DataProvider(parallel = true)` with a method returning `Object[][]` and link it to the `@Test(dataProvider = \"...\")` method","Set `RestAssured.parallel = true;`","Run multiple copies of the Eclipse IDE simultaneously","Annotate the test class with `@ParallelSuite`"],"answer":0,"explanation":"TestNG supports high-throughput parallel execution by setting `@DataProvider(parallel = true)`. TestNG invokes the `@Test` method concurrently across multiple worker threads, feeding distinct parameter sets to each thread. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2926,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"In JUnit 5 (Jupiter), which annotation is used to create parameterized, data-driven tests with REST Assured?","options":["@ParameterizedTest with @CsvSource or @MethodSource","@DataTest with @ValueSource","@TestFactory with @DataProvider","@DynamicTest with @Source"],"answer":0,"explanation":"JUnit 5 uses `@ParameterizedTest` combined with sources such as `@CsvSource`, `@ValueSource`, or `@MethodSource` to execute a single test method multiple times with different input values and expected assertions. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2927,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you perform JSON Schema validation in REST Assured to assert that a response conforms to a draft JSON schema?","options":["given().when().get(\"/users/1\").then().assertThat().body(matchesJsonSchemaInClasspath(\"user-schema.json\"));","given().when().get(\"/users/1\").then().validateJsonSchema(\"user-schema.json\");","given().when().get(\"/users/1\").then().assertSchema(\"user-schema.json\");","given().when().get(\"/users/1\").then().body(schemaEquals(\"user-schema.json\"));"],"answer":0,"explanation":"By adding the `json-schema-validator` module (`io.rest-assured:json-schema-validator`), you can use the Hamcrest matcher `matchesJsonSchemaInClasspath(\"schema.json\")` to validate response structures against JSON Schema drafts. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2928,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you validate an XML response against an XML Schema (.xsd file) in REST Assured?","options":["given().when().get(\"/order\").then().assertThat().body(matchesXsdInClasspath(\"order.xsd\"));","given().when().get(\"/order\").then().validateXsd(\"order.xsd\");","given().when().get(\"/order\").then().body(xmlSchema(\"order.xsd\"));","given().when().get(\"/order\").then().assertXsd(\"order.xsd\");"],"answer":0,"explanation":"REST Assured provides `matchesXsdInClasspath(\"file.xsd\")` or `matchesXsd(xsdString)` to validate the structural conformity of XML response payloads against XML Schema Definitions. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2929,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you extract multiple values simultaneously from a JSON response without calling `.path()` repeatedly in REST Assured?","options":["Response response = ...; JsonPath jp = response.jsonPath(); String id = jp.getString(\"id\"); String name = jp.getString(\"name\");","Use multiple given() blocks","Use System.getProperty()","Response cannot be queried more than once"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Extracting the `JsonPath` instance once via `response.jsonPath()` allows you to retrieve multiple fields (`getString()`, `getInt()`, `getList()`) efficiently without re-parsing the raw response body multiple times. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2930,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the \"ResponseSpecBuilder\" class used for in enterprise REST Assured test frameworks?","options":["Creating centralized, reusable response validation templates that assert common criteria (status 200, Content-Type, headers) across multiple test methods","Building mock responses for unit testing","Generating OpenAPI JSON specifications","Converting responses to HTML format"],"answer":0,"explanation":"`ResponseSpecBuilder` provides a builder pattern for creating reusable `ResponseSpecification` instances, allowing test suites to standardize assertions (e.g., status 200, ContentType JSON, response time under 1000ms) in one place. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2931,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the \"API Object Pattern\" (similar to Page Object Model) in API test automation frameworks?","options":["A design pattern that encapsulates HTTP request construction, endpoint paths, headers, and serialization logic into dedicated service classes, separating test logic from API mechanics","A pattern where every JSON response is stored in a relational database","A technique where all tests are written in a single monolithic class","A design pattern for converting REST endpoints into SOAP services"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The API Object Pattern abstracts individual microservice endpoints and operations into dedicated client classes (e.g., `UserService`, `OrderService`), keeping test methods clean, maintainable, and decoupled from raw HTTP plumbing. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2932,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Why is it considered an API testing best practice to keep automated test cases completely independent and self-contained?","options":["Because tests that depend on execution order or shared mutable state are fragile, prone to cascading failures, and cannot be executed in parallel","Because HTTP servers cannot process more than one request per minute","Because JSON parsers fail if test classes exceed 100 lines","Because CI runners only support single-threaded execution"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Independent, self-contained tests create their own test data (or use isolated fixtures) and clean up after themselves. This ensures tests can run in any sequence, in parallel, or in isolation without causing flakiness."},
{"id":2933,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the recommended best practice for managing dynamic test data in automated API regression test suites?","options":["Dynamically generate unique test entities via API endpoints during test setup (e.g., with timestamps/UUIDs) and tear them down after execution","Hardcode existing user IDs from the production database in test scripts","Manually insert rows into MySQL before every test run","Rely on static mock servers for all integration tests"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Creating dynamic test data programmatically via API calls or factories during `@BeforeMethod` (and cleaning up in `@AfterMethod`) ensures tests never collide on unique constraints and remain repeatable in any environment."},
{"id":2934,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Negative Testing\" in the context of API validation?","options":["Testing how the API handles invalid inputs, boundary violations, malformed payloads, unauthorized tokens, and missing headers to ensure graceful error codes (4xx) are returned","Testing the API when the server is powered off","Deleting all database tables to test recovery","Running tests with inverted color schemes on monitors"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Negative API testing validates that the system responds correctly and securely to invalid inputs, expired credentials, unauthorized roles, missing required parameters, and corrupted data by returning proper HTTP 4xx status codes and sanitized error messages."},
{"id":2935,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Boundary Value Analysis\" when applied to API parameter testing?","options":["Testing the minimum, just above minimum, nominal, just below maximum, and maximum allowable values for payload fields and parameters","Testing the physical geographic boundaries of data centers","Verifying the length of network cables connected to servers","Checking the edge pixels of generated UI screenshots"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Boundary Value Analysis tests inputs at their boundary limits (e.g., string length 0, 1, 255, 256; numeric bounds -1, 0, 100, 101) where software defects and off-by-one errors most commonly occur."},
{"id":2936,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Why should automated API tests assert response headers in addition to status codes and response bodies?","options":["To verify critical security headers (CORS, CSP, X-Content-Type-Options), caching rules (Cache-Control, ETag), and content type negotiation","Because HTTP headers determine the CPU speed of the server","Because headers contain encrypted database passwords","Because Postman cannot parse bodies without header assertions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Header validation ensures that the API properly enforces security policies (like `Strict-Transport-Security`, `X-Frame-Options`), content negotiation (`Content-Type: application/json`), and caching directives (`Cache-Control: no-store`). This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2937,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the difference between \"Smoke Testing\" and \"Regression Testing\" for APIs?","options":["Smoke testing runs a minimal set of critical endpoint checks to verify basic deployment health; regression testing runs comprehensive suites to ensure new changes didn't break existing features","Smoke testing tests database speed, while regression testing tests CSS","Smoke testing is manual, while regression testing is automated","Smoke testing only runs in production environments"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Smoke testing verifies high-level critical paths (e.g., health checks, authentication, basic entity retrieval) immediately following deployment. Regression testing executes exhaustive end-to-end assertions to safeguard against unintended regressions. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2938,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the \"Fail Fast\" principle in automated API test architecture?","options":["Halting the test run or assertion pipeline immediately when a critical precondition or sanity check fails, preventing wasted execution time","Designing servers to crash whenever an error occurs","Skipping all assertions to speed up CI pipelines","Writing tests without any assertions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Fail Fast principle ensures that if a fundamental precondition (such as login or environment availability) fails, execution halts immediately with a clear error rather than running hundreds of dependent tests destined to fail."},
{"id":2939,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is an \"API Facade\" or \"Client SDK\" in test automation architectures?","options":["A helper library that wraps low-level HTTP calls into high-level business domain methods (e.g., `client.createOrder(order)`), improving test readability and reuse","A graphical user interface for Newman","A proxy server used to block malicious web traffic","A database stored procedure for data generation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An API Facade or SDK encapsulates HTTP details (endpoints, serialization, headers, status validation) into clean, object-oriented domain methods, allowing test scripts to focus purely on business logic and assertions. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2940,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Why should API tests avoid hardcoding sensitive credentials (passwords, API tokens) in test code repositories?","options":["To prevent accidental security leaks, credential exposure in version control, and enable dynamic configuration across CI/CD environments via secrets managers","Because Java compilers reject passwords with special characters","Because Git repositories fail to commit files containing strings over 20 characters","Because Postman prohibits environment variables in cloud workspaces"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Hardcoded credentials in repositories present severe security vulnerabilities. Storing secrets in environment variables or CI/CD secret vaults (e.g., GitHub Secrets, HashiCorp Vault) keeps repositories secure and portable across environments. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2941,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is Contract Testing in modern microservice architectures?","options":["A testing methodology that verifies two separate systems (consumer and provider) have a shared agreement on the format and semantics of their API interactions","Testing the legal terms and service level agreements (SLAs) with API vendors","A test of database transactional contracts","Verifying network cable integrity between microservices"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Contract testing verifies that interacting microservices (a consumer and a provider) agree on the structure, payloads, query parameters, and status codes of their API exchanges, enabling teams to deploy independently with confidence."},
{"id":2942,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Consumer-Driven Contract Testing\" (CDCT)?","options":["A contract testing approach where consumers define their expectations in a contract, which the provider then validates against its actual implementation","A process where end users write unit tests in JavaScript","A testing technique where the backend forces schemas on the frontend","A method of automated performance testing driven by customer traffic"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Consumer-Driven Contract Testing, the API consumers define the exact requests they make and the minimal responses they expect. These expectations are published as a contract that the provider must verify against its implementation."},
{"id":2943,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Pact\" in the software testing industry?","options":["The most widely adopted open-source framework for consumer-driven contract testing across multiple programming languages","A cloud load testing platform","A relational database migration tool","A network packet capturing library for Java"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pact is a polyglot consumer-driven contract testing framework (supporting Java, JavaScript, Python, Go, C#, Ruby, etc.) that generates contract files (pacts) during consumer tests and verifies them against provider implementations."},
{"id":2944,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is a \"Pact File\"?","options":["A JSON document generated by consumer tests that records expected HTTP requests, headers, paths, bodies, and expected provider responses","A binary certificate used for SSL pinning","An OpenAPI 3.0 YAML specification","A log file produced by Newman during CI runs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Pact file is a structured JSON artifact containing the contract: the interactions (descriptions, provider states, expected HTTP request details, and expected response payloads/status codes) defined by the consumer. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2945,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the \"Pact Broker\"?","options":["A centralized service for sharing, storing, versioning, and coordinating Pact contracts and verification results between consumers and providers","A message queue used to throttle API calls","A proxy server that intercepts Postman requests","A load balancer distributing network traffic across microservices"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Pact Broker acts as the centralized repository for contract testing. It stores published contracts from consumers, provides them to provider builds for verification, records verification results, and enables \"can-i-deploy\" safety checks."},
{"id":2946,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"advanced","question":"What does the `can-i-deploy` CLI tool in Pact Broker verify in a CI/CD deployment pipeline?","options":["Whether the specific version of an application is compatible to deploy with the versions currently running in the target environment based on recorded contract verifications","Whether the cloud hosting provider has sufficient CPU capacity","Whether the git repository has any unmerged pull requests","Whether the software license fees have been paid"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pact's `can-i-deploy` CLI tool queries the Pact Broker matrix to verify that the application version you want to deploy has been successfully verified against the exact versions of all integrated services currently active in the target environment."},
{"id":2947,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is a \"Provider State\" in Pact contract testing?","options":["A named setup condition (e.g., \"user with ID 10 exists\") that the provider sets up before verifying a specific consumer interaction","The geographical location of the provider's server","The active database transaction isolation level","The HTTP status code returned by the provider"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Provider State describes the prerequisite data setup or server condition required for a test scenario (e.g., \"a product with ID 123 is out of stock\"). The provider uses state handlers to configure mock or database state accordingly before running verification."},
{"id":2948,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How does Pact Contract Testing differ from End-to-End (E2E) Integration Testing?","options":["Contract testing tests consumers and providers in complete isolation without running the entire distributed environment simultaneously, whereas E2E requires all services to be deployed and running live","Contract testing only tests UI buttons, while E2E tests databases","Contract testing is always manual, while E2E is automated","There is no difference between the two methodologies"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Contract testing eliminates the fragility, slowness, and high cost of shared E2E test environments by validating consumer-provider compatibility asynchronously in unit/component test phases without requiring live end-to-end environments. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2949,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Bi-Directional Contract Testing\" in Pact Flow?","options":["A contract testing approach where consumer contracts (Pact or mocks) and provider contracts (OpenAPI specs) are independently compared for compatibility by the broker","A technique where two developers write code simultaneously","A dual WebSocket channel test","A two-way TLS authentication handshake test"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bi-Directional Contract Testing allows teams using OpenAPI specifications on the provider side and Pact or mock-based contracts on the consumer side to verify structural compatibility in the Pact Broker without rewriting existing test suites."},
{"id":2950,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"PactumJS\"?","options":["A modern, lightweight REST API and contract testing library for Node.js","A Java ORM for MongoDB","A CSS styling framework for test dashboards","A Docker container orchestrator"],"answer":0,"explanation":"PactumJS is a next-generation, developer-friendly REST API test automation library for JavaScript and TypeScript that includes built-in support for BDD assertions, component testing, contract testing, and API mocking. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2951,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What is OpenAPI Specification (OAS, formerly Swagger Specification)?","options":["A standard, language-agnostic interface description format for RESTful APIs, written in YAML or JSON, allowing humans and computers to understand capabilities without source code access","A proprietary programming language created by Oracle","A database query optimizer for PostgreSQL","A network monitoring dashboard for Linux servers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The OpenAPI Specification (OAS) is an open, vendor-neutral standard for describing REST APIs in YAML or JSON format. It outlines available endpoints, operation parameters, authentication schemes, request bodies, and response structures."},
{"id":2952,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the primary difference between OpenAPI 2.0 (Swagger 2.0) and OpenAPI 3.0+?","options":["OpenAPI 3.0 introduced a unified `components` section, multiple server URLs, rich `requestBody` objects, improved content-type negotiation, and callbacks/webhooks","Swagger 2.0 only supported XML, while OpenAPI 3.0 only supports JSON","OpenAPI 3.0 cannot be written in YAML","Swagger 2.0 did not support GET requests"],"answer":0,"explanation":"OpenAPI 3.0 significantly restructured the specification: replacing the root `definitions` and `parameters` with a unified `components` object, introducing `servers` arrays, separating `requestBody` from path parameters, and supporting complex content negotiations."},
{"id":2953,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What is Swagger UI?","options":["An interactive web-based graphical interface that renders OpenAPI specifications as human-readable documentation with \"Try it out\" live request execution capabilities","A command-line text editor for Linux","A relational database management system","A Postman plugin for recording mobile app gestures"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Swagger UI dynamically generates an interactive web page from any OpenAPI/Swagger definition file, allowing developers and QA engineers to explore endpoints, inspect schemas, and execute real HTTP requests directly within the browser."},
{"id":2954,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is Swagger Editor?","options":["A browser-based editor for authoring and editing OpenAPI specifications with real-time linting, syntax validation, and side-by-side Swagger UI preview","A tool for editing video recordings of automated test runs","An IDE plugin for writing Java Spring Boot controllers","A compiler that converts C++ to JavaScript"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Swagger Editor is an open-source, browser-based tool for authoring OpenAPI specifications. It provides real-time schema validation, autocomplete, syntax error highlighting, and renders an immediate live preview of the generated UI documentation."},
{"id":2955,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is Swagger Codegen / OpenAPI Generator?","options":["An open-source tool that reads an OpenAPI specification and automatically generates client SDK libraries, server stubs, and API documentation in dozens of languages","A compiler that optimizes database indexes","A performance monitoring agent for Kubernetes","A tool that generates fake credit card numbers for testing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"OpenAPI Generator (and Swagger Codegen) parses OpenAPI spec files and automatically scaffolds boilerplate server stubs (e.g., Spring Boot, Express) and client SDK libraries across Java, Python, TypeScript, C#, Go, and other programming languages."},
{"id":2956,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"In an OpenAPI 3.0 specification file, what is the purpose of the `$ref` keyword?","options":["To create reusable references to schemas, parameters, responses, or request bodies defined in the `components` section or external files, avoiding duplication","To define the HTTP return status code","To specify the root server DNS address","To encrypt sensitive password fields in the document"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The `$ref` keyword uses JSON Reference syntax (e.g., `$ref: \"#/components/schemas/User\"`) to reference and reuse existing schema models and parameters across multiple endpoint definitions, maintaining DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) design. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2957,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Can Postman import an OpenAPI/Swagger specification file directly to create a collection?","options":["Yes, Postman natively imports OpenAPI YAML/JSON files to automatically generate collections with endpoints, headers, query parameters, and example schemas","No, Postman only supports importing its own proprietary JSON format","Only if the OpenAPI file is under 10KB","Only via third-party Python conversion scripts"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Postman provides native import support for OpenAPI 2.0, 3.0, and 3.1 specifications in both YAML and JSON formats, automatically generating organized collections populated with paths, parameters, schemas, and mock examples."},
{"id":2958,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What is GraphQL?","options":["An open-source data query and manipulation language for APIs, and a runtime for fulfilling queries with existing data, developed by Meta","A graph database management system like Neo4j","A JavaScript charting library for rendering graphs","A binary protocol designed to replace TCP"],"answer":0,"explanation":"GraphQL is an API specification and query language developed by Meta that allows clients to request exactly the data fields they need (preventing over-fetching and under-fetching) through a single unified endpoint via HTTP POST."},
{"id":2959,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What are the three core operation types supported by GraphQL?","options":["Query (read data), Mutation (write/modify data), and Subscription (real-time push events)","GET, POST, and DELETE","Select, Insert, and Update","Fetch, Push, and Poll"],"answer":0,"explanation":"GraphQL defines three distinct root operation types: `Query` for fetching and reading data without side effects, `Mutation` for creating, updating, or deleting data, and `Subscription` for receiving real-time event streams via WebSockets."},
{"id":2960,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How are standard GraphQL queries and mutations transmitted over HTTP?","options":["As an HTTP POST request to a single endpoint (e.g., `/graphql`) containing a JSON body with a `query` string and optional `variables` object","As multiple GET requests to distinct REST URIs","Over raw UDP sockets on port 8080","As multipart/form-data binary streams"],"answer":0,"explanation":"GraphQL typically exposes a single endpoint (such as `/graphql`) that receives HTTP POST requests. The request payload is a JSON object formatted as `{\"query\": \"...\", \"variables\": { ... }}`. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2961,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Over-fetching\" in REST APIs, and how does GraphQL solve it?","options":["Over-fetching occurs when a REST endpoint returns more data fields than the client actually needs; GraphQL solves it by allowing the client to specify exact fields in the query","Over-fetching is when a client sends too many concurrent requests","Over-fetching is an HTTP 504 timeout issue","Over-fetching refers to downloading duplicate image files"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Over-fetching happens when an endpoint returns large payloads containing unneeded fields (e.g., fetching a whole user object just to display a username). GraphQL allows clients to request only the specific attributes required for the UI view."},
{"id":2962,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Under-fetching\" and the \"N+1 Problem\" in REST, and how does GraphQL address it?","options":["Under-fetching occurs when an endpoint provides insufficient data, forcing the client to make multiple sequential round-trips; GraphQL allows fetching nested related resources in a single query","Under-fetching means the server has no internet connection","The N+1 problem occurs when databases have too many columns","Under-fetching is an out-of-memory exception in Postman"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Under-fetching requires clients to call an initial endpoint and then make N subsequent requests to fetch related sub-entities. GraphQL allows clients to request parent and nested child resources within a single consolidated query."},
{"id":2963,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the standard JSON response from a GraphQL server look like?","options":["A JSON object containing a `data` key with the requested fields, and an optional `errors` array if issues occurred","An array of HTTP status codes","A raw text string with comma delimiters","An XML SOAP envelope"],"answer":0,"explanation":"GraphQL responses always follow a standard format: an object containing a `data` property with the requested field hierarchy. If any field fails resolution, the response includes an `errors` array with error messages and location coordinates."},
{"id":2964,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Why do GraphQL responses almost always return HTTP status code 200 OK, even when execution errors occur?","options":["Because GraphQL errors are captured and returned inside the response body's `errors` array, allowing partial success where some fields succeed while others fail","Because GraphQL does not support HTTP error codes","Because GraphQL runs on UDP instead of HTTP","Because the GraphQL specification forbids status codes above 299"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because a GraphQL query can resolve multiple independent fields, a response can have partial success (returning valid `data` alongside an `errors` array). The HTTP transport layer completes successfully with 200 OK, requiring test assertions to inspect the `errors` JSON array."},
{"id":2965,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you test a GraphQL API in Postman?","options":["Select the \"GraphQL\" body type in a POST request, enter the GraphQL query/mutation, configure query variables in the variables pane, and write assertions against `pm.response.json().data`","Postman cannot send GraphQL requests","By importing a WSDL file","By using the Postman Interceptor Chrome extension only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Postman features dedicated GraphQL support: you select POST, choose the \"GraphQL\" body format, compose queries with schema autocompletion, supply JSON variables, and assert response fields via standard `pm.test` scripts. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2966,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you test a GraphQL endpoint using REST Assured in Java?","options":["given().contentType(ContentType.JSON).body(\"{\"query\":\"query { user(id: 1) { name email } }\"}\").when().post(\"/graphql\").then().statusCode(200).body(\"data.user.name\", equalTo(\"Alice\"));","RestAssured.graphql(\"query { user }\").get()","given().soapAction(\"graphql\").post(\"/graphql\")","given().graphqlQuery(\"query { user }\").fetch()"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In REST Assured, GraphQL queries are tested by sending a JSON payload containing the `query` and `variables` keys via HTTP POST to the `/graphql` route, and asserting fields using standard JsonPath like `data.user.name`."},
{"id":2967,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is gRPC, and what serialization format and transport protocol does it use by default?","options":["A high-performance open-source RPC framework developed by Google that uses Protocol Buffers (Protobuf) over HTTP/2","A RESTful JSON framework over HTTP/1.1","A WebSocket messaging broker using JSON payloads","A SOAP XML protocol over SMTP"],"answer":0,"explanation":"gRPC is a high-performance Remote Procedure Call framework developed by Google. It uses Protocol Buffers (a compact, strongly-typed binary serialization mechanism) and operates over HTTP/2 for multiplexed bidirectional streaming. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2968,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is a `.proto` file in gRPC API development and testing?","options":["An interface definition file containing service definitions, RPC methods, request messages, and response message data structures defined in Protocol Buffer syntax","A prototype JavaScript file for Newman","A configuration file for Postman monitors","A certificate file for SSL encryption"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A `.proto` file defines the contract between gRPC clients and servers. It declares service interfaces, remote procedures, and strongly-typed structured message fields (including data types and field tags). This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2969,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Can Postman be used to test gRPC APIs directly?","options":["Yes, Postman natively supports gRPC request creation, Protobuf definition loading, server reflection, and bidirectional streaming testing","No, gRPC requires compiled C++ clients exclusively","Only through Newman CLI plugins","Only if gRPC is converted to SOAP first"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Modern versions of Postman include native gRPC support, allowing users to import `.proto` files or use Server Reflection, invoke unary and streaming RPC calls, inspect message payloads, and write automated test scripts."},
{"id":2970,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What is the WebSocket protocol (RFC 6455), and how does it differ from traditional HTTP REST communication?","options":["A full-duplex, bidirectional, persistent single-TCP connection protocol that allows real-time message exchange between client and server without repeated HTTP polling overhead","A unidirectional protocol where only clients can send data","A stateless protocol that opens a new TCP connection per message","A protocol designed exclusively for transferring video files"],"answer":0,"explanation":"WebSocket provides a persistent, full-duplex communication channel over a single TCP connection. After an initial HTTP upgrade handshake (`Upgrade: websocket`), both client and server can push messages asynchronously in real time with minimal frame overhead."},
{"id":2971,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How does a WebSocket connection get established (the handshake)?","options":["The client sends an HTTP GET request with `Upgrade: websocket` and `Connection: Upgrade` headers, and the server accepts by returning HTTP status code `101 Switching Protocols`","By sending a POST request with an XML envelope","Via an FTP file upload handshake","Through an SSH port forwarding tunnel on port 22"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The WebSocket connection begins with an HTTP/1.1 handshake: the client sends a GET with headers `Upgrade: websocket`, `Connection: Upgrade`, and a `Sec-WebSocket-Key`. The server responds with `101 Switching Protocols` and `Sec-WebSocket-Accept`."},
{"id":2972,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you test WebSocket APIs in Postman?","options":["Create a new WebSocket Request (Raw or Socket.IO), connect to the `ws://` or `wss://` URL, send text/JSON/binary messages, and configure event-based test listeners","WebSockets cannot be tested in Postman","By setting the HTTP method to OPTIONS","By converting WebSocket frames to CSV files"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Postman provides native WebSocket request tabs supporting raw WebSockets and Socket.IO. Testers can connect to `ws://` or `wss://` endpoints, transmit JSON/binary messages, inspect live message streams, and write automated message-level test scripts."},
{"id":2973,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What does SOAP stand for in web services architecture?","options":["Simple Object Access Protocol","Secure Open Application Protocol","System Operations Access Procedure","Standardized Object Architecture Protocol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"SOAP stands for Simple Object Access Protocol. It is a strict, XML-based messaging protocol standard maintained by the W3C for exchanging structured information in distributed enterprise environments. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2974,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is WSDL in SOAP web services?","options":["Web Services Description Language — an XML-based language used to describe the functionality, operations, endpoints, and data contracts of a SOAP web service","Web Socket Data Link","Windows Security Directory Layer","Web System Development Logic"],"answer":0,"explanation":"WSDL (Web Services Description Language) is an XML format for describing network services as a set of endpoints operating on messages. It formally defines service operations, input/output XML message formats, and network binding details."},
{"id":2975,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What are the core structural elements of a SOAP XML Message?","options":["Envelope, Header (optional), Body, and Fault (for error details)","Request, Response, Payload, and Metadata","Schema, Class, Method, and Exception","Header, Token, Signature, and Claims"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Every SOAP message is an XML document consisting of a mandatory `<soap:Envelope>` root element, an optional `<soap:Header>` for routing and authentication metadata, a mandatory `<soap:Body>` containing application payload, and an optional `<soap:Fault>` for error reporting."},
{"id":2976,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the primary architectural difference between SOAP and REST?","options":["SOAP is a strict protocol relying exclusively on XML over HTTP/SMTP with formal WSDL contracts, while REST is an architectural style supporting multiple formats (JSON, XML) and leveraging standard HTTP verbs and status codes","SOAP is faster than REST in all mobile environments","REST only works over TCP port 443","SOAP does not support encryption"],"answer":0,"explanation":"SOAP is a rigid, standardized protocol enforcing XML schemas, WS-Security standards, and WSDL contracts. REST is a flexible architectural style emphasizing stateless resource manipulation using standard HTTP methods and lightweight JSON payloads."},
{"id":2977,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is SQL Injection (SQLi) in the context of REST API testing?","options":["A vulnerability where untrusted user input supplied via API query parameters, path variables, or request bodies is concatenated into raw database SQL queries without sanitization or parameterization","An error that occurs when SQL databases run out of disk space","A tool for backing up relational databases over HTTP","A technique for converting NoSQL databases to SQL"],"answer":0,"explanation":"SQL Injection occurs when malicious user inputs (e.g., `' OR '1'='1`) are improperly sanitized or concatenated directly into SQL execution strings, enabling attackers to bypass authentication, read sensitive records, or drop tables."},
{"id":2978,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do QA engineers test an API endpoint for SQL Injection vulnerabilities?","options":["By injecting test payloads (such as single quotes `'`, `' OR 1=1 --`, `UNION SELECT`) into query parameters and request body fields, checking whether database syntax errors or unauthorized records are returned","By running load tests with 10,000 requests per minute","By inspecting the CSS layout in web browsers","By compiling the API source code with javac"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Testers supply SQL injection vectors into input fields and parameters to verify whether the API exposes internal database error messages (like SQL syntax exceptions) or returns unauthorized data instead of returning standard HTTP 400 validation errors."},
{"id":2979,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is Broken Object Level Authorization (BOLA / IDOR) in API security (ranked #1 in OWASP API Security Top 10)?","options":["A vulnerability where an attacker accesses or manipulates another user's resource simply by changing the resource identifier (e.g., `/users/102/profile` to `/users/103/profile`) due to missing object-level ownership checks","A crash caused by null pointer exceptions in Java","An issue where JSON objects have syntax errors","A memory leak in server-side session management"],"answer":0,"explanation":"BOLA (Broken Object Level Authorization), also known as Insecure Direct Object References (IDOR), occurs when an API fails to verify whether the authenticated user actually has permission to access the specific requested object ID."},
{"id":2980,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you test an API for Broken Object Level Authorization (BOLA)?","options":["Log in as User A, obtain an authentication token, and attempt to read, update, or delete a resource owned by User B (e.g., `GET /orders/999`) using User A's token; verify the API returns HTTP 403 Forbidden","Run a security scan against the server's SSH port","Send a request without any authentication headers","Check if the API response is compressed with GZIP"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Testing for BOLA involves creating two test accounts (User A and User B). The tester authenticates as User A and attempts to perform GET/PUT/DELETE operations on resources owned by User B, asserting that the API responds with HTTP 403 Forbidden or 404 Not Found."},
{"id":2981,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is Broken User Authentication in API security?","options":["Flaws in authentication mechanisms such as weak password policies, lack of brute-force protection, missing token expiration, insecure JWT signing algorithms (`alg: none`), or credentials transmitted in URLs","A condition where the user forgets their password","A 500 error caused by database disconnection","An unhandled exception in the frontend UI login form"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Broken User Authentication encompasses implementation vulnerabilities that allow attackers to compromise passwords, hijack active session tokens, forge JWT signatures, or brute-force authentication endpoints without triggering account lockouts or rate limits."},
{"id":2982,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the \"alg: none\" JWT signature vulnerability in API authentication testing?","options":["A security flaw where the server accepts unsigned JWT tokens if the header `alg` field is altered to \"none\", completely bypassing cryptographic signature verification","A setting that makes JWT tokens execute twice as fast","An encryption mode used for public read-only APIs","A token compression format supported by OAuth 2.0"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The `alg: none` vulnerability occurs when poorly configured JWT validation libraries accept tokens where the signing algorithm is set to `none`, treating unverified, attacker-manipulated payload claims as authenticated truths. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2983,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is Broken Object Property Level Authorization (Mass Assignment / Excessive Data Exposure)?","options":["When an API automatically binds client-supplied JSON parameters to internal database models without filtering, allowing attackers to overwrite sensitive fields like `isAdmin: true` or `role: \"admin\"`","When an API returns an empty JSON response","When an endpoint requires too many query parameters","When an API exceeds its monthly hosting quota"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mass Assignment occurs when an API endpoint binds all incoming request properties directly into server domain models. Attackers exploit this to elevate privileges by injecting properties like `role: \"admin\"`, `isVerified: true`, or `balance: 99999`."},
{"id":2984,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you test for Mass Assignment vulnerabilities in an API endpoint (e.g., `PUT /api/user/profile`)?","options":["Submit a request payload containing unexpected administrative properties (e.g., `{\"name\": \"Bob\", \"role\": \"admin\", \"isSuperUser\": true}`) and verify whether the server ignores or rejects the unauthorized fields","Send an empty body with status code 200","Check if the server supports IPv6 addresses","Send 500 requests simultaneously in Postman Collection Runner"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Testers add sensitive fields (`isAdmin: true`, `accountType: \"premium\"`) to update payloads and verify that the backend ignores or rejects these unpermitted fields, confirming that only explicitly whitelisted properties are persisted."},
{"id":2985,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Excessive Data Exposure\" in REST API security?","options":["A vulnerability where an API endpoint returns complete database records containing sensitive attributes (passwords, social security numbers, internal IDs), relying solely on the client UI to filter them out","A denial of service attack caused by large payload sizes","An issue where API documentation is published on GitHub","A network error caused by uncompressed image transfers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Excessive Data Exposure happens when backend APIs serialize entire database entities into JSON responses (including hashes, PII, and internal flags) expecting client frontends to hide them, exposing sensitive data to anyone inspecting network traffic."},
{"id":2986,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) in the context of REST APIs?","options":["When an API stores unsanitized user input containing malicious HTML/JavaScript tags (e.g., `<script>alert(1)</script>`) and returns it unescaped to web clients that execute it in the victim's browser","An attack that crashes the API server hardware","A database deadlock caused by multiple concurrent transactions","An issue where API responses are cached in reverse proxies"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Stored XSS occurs when an API accepts and stores malicious JavaScript/HTML payloads without validation. When consumer applications fetch and render this data into the DOM without escaping, the script executes in the user's browser."},
{"id":2987,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS), and what is a CORS Misconfiguration vulnerability?","options":["A browser security mechanism governed by HTTP headers; a misconfiguration occurs when the server responds with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` alongside `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`, exposing authenticated data to untrusted origins","A routing rule used in microservice service meshes","An encryption standard for HTTPS connections","A database replication strategy across regions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"CORS defines how web browsers restrict cross-origin HTTP requests. Insecure configurations (e.g., reflecting arbitrary `Origin` headers with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`) allow malicious websites to steal sensitive user data via the victim's browser."},
{"id":2988,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) in API testing?","options":["A vulnerability where an attacker manipulates an API parameter (such as a webhook URL or image download link) to force the backend server to make unauthorized requests to internal network services or cloud metadata endpoints (`http://169.254.169.254`)","When a client sends requests from an invalid IP address","An attack where an attacker floods an API with DNS queries","A failure where a server redirects to an invalid 404 page"],"answer":0,"explanation":"SSRF occurs when an API accepts a remote URL from a user and fetches it without validation, enabling attackers to access internal private networks, microservices, or cloud instance metadata services (like AWS IMDS at `169.254.169.254`)."},
{"id":2989,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is Rate Limiting / Unrestricted Resource Consumption in API security?","options":["The absence of request throttling, allowing attackers to perform denial of service (DoS) attacks, brute-force passwords, or exhaust cloud compute and billing budgets through high-frequency requests","A rule that restricts the size of database disk storage","A network failure caused by slow Wi-Fi routers","An error caused by exceeding JavaScript memory limits"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Unrestricted Resource Consumption happens when APIs do not enforce rate limits (requests per minute/hour), allowing attackers to overwhelm compute resources, execute brute-force dictionary attacks, or inflate infrastructure costs. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2990,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is Broken Function Level Authorization (BFLA) in API security testing?","options":["When regular users can execute administrative endpoints (e.g., `DELETE /api/admin/users/45` or `GET /api/v1/export-all`) due to missing role-based access control (RBAC) checks on specific routes","A compilation error inside Java service methods","A syntax error in GraphQL query strings","A failure where an API endpoint returns HTTP 500"],"answer":0,"explanation":"BFLA occurs when access control checks are improperly implemented at the route or controller level, allowing standard non-privileged users to access sensitive administrative endpoints by simply guessing or predicting administrative URLs."},
{"id":2991,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do QA engineers test for Broken Function Level Authorization (BFLA)?","options":["Log in with a low-privileged user account (e.g., \"Member\") and attempt to send requests to administrative endpoints (e.g., `POST /api/admin/roles`); assert that the server returns HTTP 403 Forbidden","Check whether the admin panel has attractive styling","Send requests using Newman with `--bail` flag","Verify that passwords contain at least 8 characters"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Testing for BFLA involves attempting to invoke administrative and privileged API functions using the credentials or access token of a standard, low-privilege user, verifying that the server strictly rejects the request with HTTP 403 Forbidden."},
{"id":2992,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Security Misconfiguration\" in API deployments?","options":["Leaving default credentials active, exposing detailed stack traces and debug logs in error responses, enabling unnecessary HTTP methods, or omitting essential security headers","Configuring automated tests to run at night","Using open-source testing libraries like REST Assured","Deploying APIs across multiple availability zones"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Security misconfigurations include unpatched servers, default administrative passwords, exposed diagnostic endpoints (`/actuator/env`, `/debug`), verbose stack traces exposing database queries, and missing security headers like HSTS. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2993,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Command Injection\" (OS Command Injection) in API testing?","options":["A critical vulnerability where an API passes unsanitized user inputs directly into operating system shell execution commands (e.g., `exec(\"ping \" + host)`), allowing attackers to execute arbitrary system commands","An error where git commands fail in CI pipelines","An issue where Newman commands have invalid flags","A database transaction timeout error"],"answer":0,"explanation":"OS Command Injection occurs when an API passes untrusted input directly into system shells without sanitization. Attackers can append shell metacharacters (e.g., `; cat /etc/passwd` or `| whoami`) to take full control of the underlying host server."},
{"id":2994,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"XML External Entity\" (XXE) injection in SOAP and XML-based REST APIs?","options":["An attack against XML parsers where malicious XML payloads define external entities that force the parser to read local server files (`file:///etc/passwd`) or initiate internal network connections","An error when XML tags are not properly closed","A failure in JSON to XML serialization","A syntax error in XPath query expressions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"XXE injection exploits weakly configured XML parsers that process External Entity references. Attackers craft XML payloads containing `<!DOCTYPE foo [ <!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM \"file:///etc/passwd\"> ]>` to read confidential files or trigger SSRF attacks."},
{"id":2995,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do modern API backends prevent XML External Entity (XXE) attacks?","options":["By completely disabling Document Type Definitions (DTDs) and external entity resolution (`disallow-doctype-decl = true`) in the server-side XML parser configuration","By compressing all XML files with ZIP algorithms","By converting all incoming XML files into YAML","By limiting XML payload sizes to 500 bytes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The most effective remediation for XXE vulnerabilities is configuring server XML parsers to completely disable DTD parsing and external entity resolution features (e.g., `setFeature(\"http://apache.org/xml/features/disallow-doctype-decl\", true)`). This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2996,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Content-Type Sniffing\", and which HTTP response header prevents it?","options":["When browsers inspect the MIME content of a file rather than trusting the declared Content-Type header; prevented by the `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` header","When an API server inspects incoming JSON payloads","When Postman auto-detects request body formats","When Newman parses JUnit XML test reports"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Content-Type Sniffing allows browsers to execute files containing disguised executable scripts. Serving the `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` response header forces browsers to strictly adhere to the MIME type specified in the `Content-Type` header."},
{"id":2997,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of the `Strict-Transport-Security` (HSTS) response header in API security?","options":["It instructs web browsers and HTTP clients to strictly communicate with the server exclusively over HTTPS for a designated period, preventing SSL-stripping attacks","It enforces strict password complexity on user logins","It restricts API traffic to a specific IP subnet","It forces JSON payloads to be encrypted with PGP"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains` header enforces mandatory HTTPS connections, instructing clients never to send unencrypted HTTP requests to the domain even if requested by links. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2998,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Improper Assets Management\" in the OWASP API Security Top 10?","options":["Exposing outdated, unpatched, or deprecated API versions (e.g., `/v1/users` alongside `/v2/users`) that lack modern security controls and authentication mechanisms","Losing physical laptop assets containing test code","Having too many open pull requests in GitHub","Running tests on outdated operating systems"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Improper Assets Management occurs when legacy or debug API versions, staging endpoints, or shadow APIs remain accessible on public networks without the security patches, rate limiting, and access controls applied to current production versions."},
{"id":2999,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"API Fuzzing\" in automated security testing?","options":["An automated testing technique that injects massive volumes of semi-random, unexpected, boundary, and malformed inputs into API endpoints to discover memory leaks, unhandled exceptions, and security vulnerabilities","A method of formatting JavaScript code with Prettier","A tool for generating fake user profile pictures","A process for cleaning up expired database records"],"answer":0,"explanation":"API Fuzzing sends thousands of mutated, unexpected, oversized, or invalid input payloads to API endpoints to uncover unhandled crashes, assertion panics, buffer overflows, and input validation vulnerabilities. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":3000,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is OWASP ZAP (Zed Attack Proxy), and how is it used in API testing?","options":["An open-source web application and API security scanner that can act as an intercepting proxy to perform automated vulnerability scans against REST, SOAP, and OpenAPI endpoints","A high-speed load testing tool developed in Go","A continuous delivery orchestrator for Docker containers","A unit testing framework for JavaScript"],"answer":0,"explanation":"OWASP ZAP is an open-source dynamic application security testing (DAST) tool. It imports OpenAPI definitions, proxies API traffic, and launches automated active/passive security vulnerability scans against target endpoints. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":3001,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is an \"Intercepting Proxy\" (such as Burp Suite or OWASP ZAP) in API security assessment?","options":["A security tool that sits between the client and server to capture, inspect, modify, and replay HTTP/HTTPS requests and responses in real time","A network router that assigns DHCP addresses","A reverse proxy that caches static images for CDN networks","A database connection pool manager"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An intercepting proxy captures live HTTP/HTTPS traffic between the client and API backend, allowing security engineers and QA testers to inspect headers, alter request parameters on the fly, and probe for vulnerabilities."},
{"id":3002,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"JSON Injection\" or Parameter Tampering in API security?","options":["An attack where an attacker manipulates JSON structures by injecting duplicate keys or special formatting characters to alter backend business logic","A method for validating JSON schemas in Postman","A tool for converting XML payloads into JSON","A technique for serializing Java objects to strings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"JSON Injection occurs when untrusted input is concatenated into JSON strings without proper escaping, allowing attackers to inject additional key-value pairs or alter data parsing semantics in downstream services. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":3003,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of testing \"HTTP Parameter Pollution\" (HPP) on APIs?","options":["Testing how the API behaves when multiple parameters with the same name are supplied (e.g., `?id=10&id=20`), verifying whether backend frameworks process the first, last, or an array of values securely","Checking if water damage affects network servers","Verifying if query strings exceed 100 characters","Testing if URL encoding uses uppercase letters"],"answer":0,"explanation":"HTTP Parameter Pollution tests how web applications handle duplicate parameter names. Inconsistent parsing between frontend proxies and backend servers can lead to authentication bypass or logic flaws. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":3004,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is an \"Authentication Bypass\" vulnerability in RESTful APIs?","options":["A critical flaw allowing unauthenticated callers to access protected endpoints without presenting valid credentials or tokens, often due to routing misconfigurations or logic flaws in middleware","When a user enters an incorrect password","When an OAuth token expires after 1 hour","When a server returns HTTP 401 Unauthorized"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Authentication Bypass flaws occur when security filters fail to execute on certain routes (e.g., path traversal like `/api/v1/..;/admin/settings` or case-sensitivity mismatches), giving unauthenticated attackers full access to private resources. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":3005,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Path Traversal\" (Directory Traversal) in API file download endpoints?","options":["A vulnerability where an attacker manipulates file path parameters using dot-dot-slash sequences (e.g., `GET /download?file=../../../../etc/passwd`) to access unauthorized files on the server filesystem","A routing algorithm used in GPS software","A method for navigating folders in Postman","A database indexing technique for tree structures"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Path Traversal vulnerabilities allow attackers to bypass intended folder restrictions and access sensitive operating system files, configuration files, and source code by passing `../` directory traversal characters in file parameters."},
{"id":3006,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you test an API endpoint for Path Traversal vulnerabilities?","options":["Supply values like `../../../../etc/passwd` or `..\\..\\Windows\\win.ini` in file name parameters and verify whether the server strictly rejects the request or sanitizes the path","Send requests with large payload sizes over 10MB","Check if the API supports HTTP/2 protocol","Test the API response time using REST Assured time matchers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Testing for Path Traversal involves injecting relative path traversal sequences into input parameters that reference filenames, verifying that the server rejects directory navigation and returns HTTP 400 or 403. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":3007,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Replay Attack\" vulnerability in API transaction processing?","options":["An attack where an interceptor captures a valid network transmission (e.g., a funds transfer request) and maliciously re-transmits it repeatedly to duplicate the transaction","When an automated test script is rerun twice in Jenkins","When a server restarts after a scheduled maintenance","When Postman Collection Runner loops 10 times"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Replay Attack occurs when an attacker captures a legitimate API request and re-sends it to execute unauthorized duplicate actions. APIs protect against this using nonces, unique transaction IDs, and short-lived timestamped HMAC signatures."},
{"id":3008,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Timing Attack\" vulnerability in API password or token verification?","options":["A side-channel attack where an attacker measures slight differences in server response times (due to non-constant-time string comparisons) to guess valid characters of tokens or passwords","When an API test fails due to a network timeout","When an API server clock is out of sync with NTP","When a load test runs for 24 hours consecutively"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Timing attacks exploit non-constant-time comparisons (which terminate on the first mismatched byte). By precisely measuring server response latency down to microseconds, attackers can determine correct characters in cryptographic tokens. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":3009,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Information Disclosure\" via API error messages?","options":["When an API response returns raw database stack traces, SQL syntax error logs, server software version numbers, or internal IP addresses to the client upon failure","When an API documentation website is made public","When an API returns a valid 200 OK status code","When a user shares their email address in a profile form"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Information Disclosure occurs when APIs leak sensitive implementation details (database tables, framework versions, internal paths, raw exception traces) in error responses, providing attackers with actionable intelligence to exploit vulnerabilities. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":3010,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is a \"Slowloris\" attack in API availability testing?","options":["A denial of service attack where an attacker opens numerous concurrent HTTP connections to the API server and sends partial request headers very slowly, exhausting the server's thread pool","A tool for testing slow database queries","A unit test that takes more than 10 seconds to execute","A slow network connection between client and router"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Slowloris is a low-bandwidth DoS attack that keeps HTTP connections open by sending partial HTTP headers at slow intervals, holding web server connection pools hostage until legitimate users can no longer connect."},
{"id":3011,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"API Scraping\" and how do organizations protect public APIs from automated scraping bots?","options":["Automated extraction of massive amounts of data from public endpoints; mitigated using rate limiting, CAPTCHAs, bot management solutions, API keys, and IP reputation scoring","Deleting old API documentation pages","A tool for cleaning up test database tables","A method for validating JSON schema formats"],"answer":0,"explanation":"API scraping involves automated harvesting of proprietary business data (e.g., pricing, catalog items) at scale. Organizations mitigate this using rate limiting, behavioral bot detection, Web Application Firewalls (WAF), and mandatory API keys."},
{"id":3012,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"JWT Algorithm Confusion\" (e.g., RS256 vs HS256)?","options":["A vulnerability where an API expecting an RSA public-key signed token (RS256) is tricked into verifying an HMAC token (HS256) signed with the public key treated as a symmetric secret","An issue where JWT tokens contain invalid characters","A failure where JWT tokens expire too quickly","A bug in Base64 decoding libraries"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Algorithm confusion occurs when a backend that uses asymmetric RS256 accepts symmetric HS256 tokens signed by an attacker using the server's public key as the HMAC secret, successfully forging valid administrative tokens."},
{"id":3013,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of the `Content-Security-Policy` (CSP) HTTP header?","options":["To restrict the sources (scripts, styles, images, frames) that the browser is allowed to load and execute, mitigating Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) and data injection attacks","To encrypt server database files on disk","To validate JSON schemas against database models","To set the maximum execution timeout for API calls"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Content-Security-Policy (CSP) is a powerful HTTP header that restricts where resources (scripts, stylesheets, frames) can be loaded from, effectively preventing malicious script execution and clickjacking attacks. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":3014,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the role of an API Gateway in API security architecture?","options":["Serving as a centralized entry point that enforces authentication, SSL termination, rate limiting, request validation, CORS policies, and DDoS protection across backend microservices","A hardware cable connecting two database servers","A desktop IDE for writing automated test scripts","A plugin for formatting JSON responses in browsers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An API Gateway (such as Kong, Apigee, or AWS API Gateway) acts as the single reverse proxy ingress point, centralizing authentication, rate limiting, request inspection, routing, logging, and security policy enforcement."},
{"id":3015,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Clickjacking\" and which HTTP response header protects against it?","options":["An attack that tricks a user into clicking a hidden or transparent iframe button on an external site; prevented by the `X-Frame-Options: DENY` (or `SAMEORIGIN`) header and CSP `frame-ancestors`","An automated tool for clicking UI buttons rapidly","A mouse tracking hardware malfunction","A load testing metric measuring button clicks"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Clickjacking embeds a vulnerable website within a transparent `<iframe>` overlaying an attacker's decoy site. Setting `X-Frame-Options: DENY` or `frame-ancestors 'none'` prevents browsers from rendering the page inside iframes. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":3016,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Why should API responses containing sensitive user data include the header `Cache-Control: no-store`?","options":["To instruct browsers, intermediate proxies, and CDNs never to store any version of the response in cache memory or disk, preventing unauthorized data leakage","To make API responses transfer faster across networks","To force the server to recalculate mathematical formulas","To prevent database tables from fragmenting"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The `Cache-Control: no-store, no-cache, must-revalidate` directive guarantees that sensitive financial or personal data is never written to disk or shared caches, protecting users on shared workstations from credential and data exposure."},
{"id":3017,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Header Injection\" (CRLF Injection) in HTTP API testing?","options":["An attack where Carriage Return (`\\r`) and Line Feed (`\\n`) characters are injected into user input that gets reflected into response headers, allowing attackers to inject arbitrary headers or split HTTP responses","A technique for passing authorization tokens in headers","A tool for inspecting network packets in Wireshark","A method for validating response status codes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"CRLF Injection exploits insufficient validation of input written into response headers. By injecting `\\r\\n`, attackers can terminate the header section prematurely, inject malicious headers (like `Set-Cookie`), or attach unauthorized response bodies."},
{"id":3018,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Session Fixation\" in API session management?","options":["A vulnerability where an attacker forces a victim to use a known session identifier, allowing the attacker to hijack the session once the victim successfully authenticates","When a user session never expires","A method for fixing bugs in session management code","A database deadlock occurring during session creation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Session Fixation occurs when an application maintains the same session ID before and after user authentication. Attackers provide a victim with a pre-set session ID and subsequently access the victim's account once login succeeds."},
{"id":3019,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is \"Unvalidated Redirects and Forwards\" in API authentication flows?","options":["A vulnerability where an API accepts an arbitrary, unvalidated URL parameter (e.g., `?redirect_url=http://malicious.com`) after login, redirecting users to phishing websites","When an HTTP 301 redirect points to an internal page","When an API endpoint moves to a new domain name","When a load balancer forwards traffic to a backup server"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Open Redirect vulnerabilities allow attackers to construct legitimate-looking links that redirect authenticated users to phishing or malware websites after completing authentication on the genuine API domain. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":3020,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the primary role of Static Application Security Testing (SAST) vs Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST) in API testing?","options":["SAST analyzes API source code for vulnerabilities at rest without executing it; DAST tests running API endpoints from the outside by sending live requests and analyzing responses","SAST is for manual testing; DAST is for automated testing","SAST is used only for mobile apps; DAST is for web apps","SAST tests performance; DAST tests databases"],"answer":0,"explanation":"SAST (Static Analysis) scans raw source code, configurations, and dependencies for security flaws during build time. DAST (Dynamic Analysis, like OWASP ZAP) interacts with live, running API instances over the network to detect runtime vulnerabilities."},
]);
